# im an obsessive learner — for domain invariants 🦉📜

a **zoom-in** of `im_an.obsessive_learner` onto one lesson kind: *what must always hold true in this domain*.

an invariant is a must-hold rule — a biconditional or constraint the domain guarantees. it lands in its own `define.invariant.<scope>.md` brief. peer of `.for.domain.terms`: that captures the **words**, this captures the **rules**.

extant: `define.invariant.review.peer.passage` (`PASS ⟺ every peer guard is terminal`) · `define.invariant.review.peer.exhausted`.

## ⚠️ .label the KIND — nature or nurture. it is the first field

| kind | it holds because | to overturn it, show | NOT admissible |
|---|---|---|---|
| **nature** | of what the domain **is**; no decision could have gone otherwise | the domain was **misread** | *"this is inconvenient"* |
| **nurture** | we **chose** it, usually paid for in incidents, and choose it still | the incidents no longer apply, or the cost changed | *"i would have chosen differently"* |

nature: *a machine is never blank* — every box ships a shell, a PATH, dotfiles, so no run ever puts an environment on an empty machine; it always raises an extant one. that is why `upgrade` is accurate and `install` misleads. **a fact about machines, not a preference.**

nurture: *one entrypoint* — nature permits many; four incidents taught us they drift.

⇒ **without the label, every invariant gets argued as if it were nurture** — negotiable on taste — even the ones that are simply facts. an unlabelled nature fact loses to whichever word is more convenient.

## .the shape

1. **.what** — the rule in one line, imperative
2. **.kind** — `nature` or `nurture`, plus one sentence on why
3. **.invariant** — stated formally (`A ⟺ B`, or a constraint)
4. **.why** — why the domain depends on it; often a past incident
5. 🔴 **.scope** — what it does **NOT** cover
6. **.the litigation** — who argued what, and what settled it. quote the human **verbatim**
7. 🔴 **.the counter-argument** — the strongest case against, stated fairly
8. 🔴 **.what would overturn it** — per the nature/nurture table
9. **.enforcement** — what a violation is

⚠️ **fields 5, 7, 8 are the three a conclusion-only rule always omits**, and the three a re-litigation needs. **the second argument will not have the evidence the first one had.**

**the case that proves `.scope` carries weight.** a robot read *"two implementations drift"* (true, hard-won over four incidents) and applied it to a transport pair that carried one payload two ways. the human stopped it: *"wait. is rsync the main usecase though? if so, i get the fallback. just document it clearly why we need both, so we dont relitigate."* **the rule was right and the application was wrong, because the rule's SCOPE had never been written down.**

⇒ *"so we dont relitigate"* is the requirement. a conclusion with no argument attached will be re-argued — or quietly reversed by someone who only ever saw the conclusion.

## 🔴 .a settled invariant is NOT deferrable

did this round **settle** a must-hold rule? → it gets its brief **this round**. deferral is valid only for a rule still under dispute or one that needs deeper discovery — **never** for one already settled.

📜 a **litigated** invariant is the highest-value kind: the argument is worth more than the conclusion, and **the argument fades faster**.

> a regression is an invariant that was never declared. declare it while the round is warm. 🦉📜💎
